# WebAssembly programs

## About this notebook

`beam.com` runs WebAssembly modules and WASI programs. A program calls the module `wasm`: `wasm:compile/1`, `wasm:instantiate/3`, `wasm:call_function/3` and `wasm:run/2` (see [Libraries](../LIBRARIES.md)).

In the native file, WAMR (the WebAssembly Micro Runtime, linked into `beam.com`) runs the modules. This Livebook runs in the WebAssembly runtime of beam.com, which has no WAMR. There, the module `wasm` sends each call to the WebAssembly engine of the host: the engine of your browser in this page, and V8 on Deno. The API is the same, so the calls of `wasm` in this notebook also run in a native `beam.com`.

In this notebook:

* An assembler, in Elixir, makes a module from WebAssembly text.
* The cells call the functions of the module, and read and write its memory.
* A C program, compiled for WASI, runs with its arguments and its environment.
* An Erlang cell does the same calls.

```elixir
Code.ensure_loaded?(:wasm) and function_exported?(:wasm, :run, 2)
```

## From text to bytes

The WebAssembly text format (WAT) is the text form of a module. Tools such as `wat2wasm` change it to the binary format. This Livebook has no such tool, so the next cell is a small assembler in Elixir. It knows a part of the format:

* functions with `i32` and `i64` values, their parameters, their locals and their exports;
* the flat form of the instructions: `block`, `loop`, `if`, `else`, `end`, `br`, `br_if`, `call`, the locals, the constants, the arithmetic, the comparisons, the loads and the stores;
* one memory, with its export and its data.

The binary format is a list of sections. Each section has an id, a size and its content. The numbers are LEB128: 7 bits in each byte.

```elixir
defmodule Wat do
  @moduledoc """
  An assembler for a part of the WebAssembly text format (WAT): the
  functions (i32 and i64), their exports, one memory and its data. The
  instructions are in the flat form (one instruction after the other),
  not in the folded form.
  """

  import Bitwise

  @types %{"i32" => 0x7F, "i64" => 0x7E, "f32" => 0x7D, "f64" => 0x7C}

  # The instructions with no immediate value.
  @plain %{
    "unreachable" => 0x00, "nop" => 0x01, "return" => 0x0F, "drop" => 0x1A,
    "select" => 0x1B,
    "i32.eqz" => 0x45, "i32.eq" => 0x46, "i32.ne" => 0x47, "i32.lt_s" => 0x48,
    "i32.lt_u" => 0x49, "i32.gt_s" => 0x4A, "i32.gt_u" => 0x4B, "i32.le_s" => 0x4C,
    "i32.le_u" => 0x4D, "i32.ge_s" => 0x4E, "i32.ge_u" => 0x4F,
    "i64.eqz" => 0x50, "i64.eq" => 0x51, "i64.ne" => 0x52, "i64.lt_s" => 0x53,
    "i64.lt_u" => 0x54, "i64.gt_s" => 0x55, "i64.gt_u" => 0x56, "i64.le_s" => 0x57,
    "i64.le_u" => 0x58, "i64.ge_s" => 0x59, "i64.ge_u" => 0x5A,
    "i32.add" => 0x6A, "i32.sub" => 0x6B, "i32.mul" => 0x6C, "i32.div_s" => 0x6D,
    "i32.div_u" => 0x6E, "i32.rem_s" => 0x6F, "i32.rem_u" => 0x70, "i32.and" => 0x71,
    "i32.or" => 0x72, "i32.xor" => 0x73, "i32.shl" => 0x74, "i32.shr_s" => 0x75,
    "i32.shr_u" => 0x76,
    "i64.add" => 0x7C, "i64.sub" => 0x7D, "i64.mul" => 0x7E, "i64.div_s" => 0x7F,
    "i64.div_u" => 0x80, "i64.rem_s" => 0x81, "i64.rem_u" => 0x82, "i64.and" => 0x83,
    "i64.or" => 0x84, "i64.xor" => 0x85, "i64.shl" => 0x86, "i64.shr_s" => 0x87,
    "i64.shr_u" => 0x88,
    "i32.wrap_i64" => 0xA7, "i64.extend_i32_s" => 0xAC, "i64.extend_i32_u" => 0xAD
  }

  # The loads and stores: the opcode and the alignment (log2 of the bytes).
  @memory %{
    "i32.load" => {0x28, 2}, "i64.load" => {0x29, 3}, "i32.load8_u" => {0x2D, 0},
    "i32.store" => {0x36, 2}, "i64.store" => {0x37, 3}, "i32.store8" => {0x3A, 0}
  }

  @doc "The bytes of the module in the text `source`."
  def assemble(source) do
    [["module" | fields]] = source |> tokens() |> parse()
    funcs = for ["func" | rest] <- fields, do: func(rest)
    index = for {f, i} <- Enum.with_index(funcs), f.name, into: %{}, do: {f.name, i}
    types = funcs |> Enum.map(& &1.type) |> Enum.uniq()
    memories = for ["memory" | rest] <- fields, do: rest

    exports =
      for({f, i} <- Enum.with_index(funcs), name <- f.exports, do: {name, 0, i}) ++
        for rest <- memories, ["export", name] <- rest, do: {name, 2, 0}

    IO.iodata_to_binary([
      "\0asm", <<1, 0, 0, 0>>,
      section(1, vec(types, fn {params, results} ->
        [0x60, vec(params, &@types[&1]), vec(results, &@types[&1])]
      end)),
      section(3, vec(funcs, &u(Enum.find_index(types, fn t -> t == &1.type end)))),
      section(5, vec(memories, &limits(for n <- &1, is_binary(n), do: number(n)))),
      section(7, vec(exports, fn {name, kind, i} -> [name(name), kind, u(i)] end)),
      section(10, vec(funcs, &code(&1, index))),
      section(11, vec(for(["data" | rest] <- fields, do: rest), fn [["i32.const", at] | text] ->
        [0, 0x41, s(number(at)), 0x0B, name(Enum.map_join(text, fn {:text, t} -> t end))]
      end))
    ])
  end

  # --- the text: tokens, then nested lists ---------------------------------

  defp tokens(source) do
    ~r/\(|\)|"(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*"|[^\s()";]+|;;[^\n]*/
    |> Regex.scan(source)
    |> Enum.map(&hd/1)
    |> Enum.reject(&String.starts_with?(&1, ";;"))
  end

  defp parse(tokens), do: tokens |> nest([]) |> elem(0)

  defp nest([], acc), do: {Enum.reverse(acc), []}
  defp nest([")" | rest], acc), do: {Enum.reverse(acc), rest}

  defp nest(["(" | rest], acc) do
    {list, rest} = nest(rest, [])
    nest(rest, [list | acc])
  end

  defp nest(["\"" <> _ = text | rest], acc), do: nest(rest, [{:text, unquote_text(text)} | acc])
  defp nest([token | rest], acc), do: nest(rest, [token | acc])

  # A string: \n, \t, \\, \" and \HH (a byte in hexadecimal).
  defp unquote_text(text) do
    text
    |> String.slice(1..-2//1)
    |> then(&Regex.replace(~r/\\([0-9a-fA-F]{2}|.)/, &1, fn _, e ->
      case e do
        "n" -> "\n"
        "t" -> "\t"
        <<_, _>> -> <<String.to_integer(e, 16)>>
        other -> other
      end
    end))
  end

  # --- a function ------------------------------------------------------------

  defp func(rest) do
    {name, rest} =
      case rest do
        ["$" <> _ = name | rest] -> {name, rest}
        rest -> {nil, rest}
      end

    {header, body} = Enum.split_while(rest, &is_list/1)
    params = for ["param" | p] <- header, do: p
    locals = for ["local" | l] <- header, do: l

    %{
      name: name,
      exports: for(["export", {:text, e}] <- header, do: e),
      type: {types(params), for(["result" | r] <- header, t <- r, do: t)},
      locals: types(locals),
      # The names of the parameters and of the locals, to their index.
      names: (params ++ locals) |> Enum.flat_map(&names/1) |> Enum.with_index() |> Map.new(),
      body: body
    }
  end

  # (param $a i32) has a name; (param i32 i32) has none.
  defp names(["$" <> _ = name, _type]), do: [name]
  defp names(types), do: Enum.map(types, fn _ -> nil end)

  defp types(decls), do: for(d <- decls, t <- d, Map.has_key?(@types, t), do: t)

  defp code(f, funcs) do
    locals = f.locals |> Enum.chunk_by(& &1) |> Enum.map(&[u(length(&1)), @types[hd(&1)]])
    body = [vec(locals, & &1), instructions(f.body, f.names, funcs, []), 0x0B]
    [u(IO.iodata_length(body)), body]
  end

  # labels: the names of the open blocks, the innermost first (for br).
  defp instructions([], _names, _funcs, _labels), do: []

  defp instructions([op | rest], names, funcs, labels) when op in ["block", "loop", "if"] do
    {label, rest} =
      case rest do
        ["$" <> _ = label | rest] -> {label, rest}
        rest -> {nil, rest}
      end

    {type, rest} =
      case rest do
        [["result", t] | rest] -> {@types[t], rest}
        rest -> {0x40, rest}
      end

    opcode = %{"block" => 0x02, "loop" => 0x03, "if" => 0x04}[op]
    [opcode, type | instructions(rest, names, funcs, [label | labels])]
  end

  defp instructions(["else" | rest], n, f, l), do: [0x05 | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]
  defp instructions(["end" | rest], n, f, [_ | l]), do: [0x0B | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]

  defp instructions([op, target | rest], n, f, l) when op in ["br", "br_if"] do
    depth = if String.starts_with?(target, "$"), do: Enum.find_index(l, &(&1 == target)), else: number(target)
    [if(op == "br", do: 0x0C, else: 0x0D), u(depth) | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]
  end

  defp instructions(["call", target | rest], n, f, l) do
    i = if String.starts_with?(target, "$"), do: Map.fetch!(f, target), else: number(target)
    [0x10, u(i) | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]
  end

  defp instructions([op, local | rest], n, f, l)
       when op in ["local.get", "local.set", "local.tee"] do
    i = if String.starts_with?(local, "$"), do: Map.fetch!(n, local), else: number(local)
    opcode = %{"local.get" => 0x20, "local.set" => 0x21, "local.tee" => 0x22}[op]
    [opcode, u(i) | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]
  end

  defp instructions(["i32.const", v | rest], n, f, l), do: [0x41, s(number(v)) | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]
  defp instructions(["i64.const", v | rest], n, f, l), do: [0x42, s(number(v)) | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]
  defp instructions(["memory.size" | rest], n, f, l), do: [0x3F, 0 | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]
  defp instructions(["memory.grow" | rest], n, f, l), do: [0x40, 0 | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]

  defp instructions([op | rest], n, f, l) do
    case {@plain[op], @memory[op]} do
      {opcode, nil} when opcode != nil ->
        [opcode | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]

      {nil, {opcode, align}} ->
        # The offset: "offset=N" after the instruction, or 0.
        {offset, rest} =
          case rest do
            ["offset=" <> o | rest] -> {number(o), rest}
            rest -> {0, rest}
          end

        [opcode, u(align), u(offset) | instructions(rest, n, f, l)]

      _ ->
        raise ArgumentError, "unknown instruction: #{inspect(op)}"
    end
  end

  # --- the binary format -----------------------------------------------------

  defp section(_id, [[0]]), do: []
  defp section(id, content), do: [id, u(IO.iodata_length(content)), content]

  # The size of a memory in pages of 64 KiB: the minimum, and the maximum.
  defp limits([min]), do: [0, u(min)]
  defp limits([min, max]), do: [1, u(min), u(max)]

  defp vec(items, fun), do: [u(length(items)) | Enum.map(items, fun)]
  defp name({:text, text}), do: name(text)
  defp name(text), do: [u(byte_size(text)), text]

  defp number("0x" <> hex), do: String.to_integer(String.replace(hex, "_", ""), 16)
  defp number("-0x" <> hex), do: -number("0x" <> hex)
  defp number(text), do: String.to_integer(String.replace(text, "_", ""))

  # LEB128: 7 bits in each byte, the low bits first; the high bit of a
  # byte tells that more bytes come.
  defp u(n) when n < 0x80, do: [n]
  defp u(n), do: [0x80 ||| (n &&& 0x7F) | u(n >>> 7)]

  defp s(n) do
    low = n &&& 0x7F
    rest = n >>> 7

    if (rest == 0 and (low &&& 0x40) == 0) or (rest == -1 and (low &&& 0x40) != 0),
      do: [low],
      else: [0x80 ||| low | s(rest)]
  end
end
```

## A module

Change the text and run the cells again. The module has five functions:

* `add` adds two `i32` values.
* `fact` gives n! with `i64` values, by recursion.
* `sum` adds the bytes of the memory, with a loop.
* `pages` grows the memory with `memory.grow`.
* `divide` divides two `i32` values.

```elixir
source =
  Kino.Input.textarea("WebAssembly text",
    monospace: true,
    default: ~S"""
    (module
      ;; The memory: one page of 64 KiB (4 at most), with a text at 0.
      (memory (export "memory") 1 4)
      (data (i32.const 0) "Hello, memory!\n")

      (func $add (export "add") (param $a i32) (param $b i32) (result i32)
        local.get $a
        local.get $b
        i32.add)

      ;; n! with 64-bit integers, by recursion.
      (func $fact (export "fact") (param $n i64) (result i64)
        local.get $n
        i64.const 1
        i64.le_s
        if (result i64)
          i64.const 1
        else
          local.get $n
          local.get $n
          i64.const 1
          i64.sub
          call $fact
          i64.mul
        end)

      ;; The sum of len bytes at ptr, with a loop.
      (func (export "sum") (param $ptr i32) (param $len i32) (result i32)
        (local $total i32)
        block $done
          loop $next
            local.get $len
            i32.eqz
            br_if $done
            local.get $total
            local.get $ptr
            i32.load8_u
            i32.add
            local.set $total
            local.get $ptr
            i32.const 1
            i32.add
            local.set $ptr
            local.get $len
            i32.const -1
            i32.add
            local.set $len
            br $next
          end
        end
        local.get $total)

      (func (export "pages") (param $more i32) (result i32)
        local.get $more
        memory.grow)

      (func (export "divide") (param i32 i32) (result i32)
        local.get 0
        local.get 1
        i32.div_s))
    """
  )
```

```elixir
bytes = Wat.assemble(Kino.Input.read(source))
"#{byte_size(bytes)} bytes"
```

The bytes start with `\0asm` and the version 1. Then come the sections. This reader shows them:

```elixir
defmodule WasmBytes do
  @moduledoc "The sections of a WebAssembly module, as the binary format gives them."

  import Bitwise

  @names %{
    0 => "custom", 1 => "type", 2 => "import", 3 => "function", 4 => "table",
    5 => "memory", 6 => "global", 7 => "export", 8 => "start", 9 => "element",
    10 => "code", 11 => "data", 12 => "data count"
  }

  @doc "Each section: its name, its offset in the file, its size and its first bytes."
  def sections(<<"\0asm", 1::little-32, rest::binary>>), do: read(rest, 8)

  defp read(<<>>, _at), do: []

  defp read(<<id, rest::binary>>, at) do
    {size, rest, length} = uleb(rest)
    <<content::binary-size(^size), rest::binary>> = rest

    section = %{
      section: @names[id],
      offset: at,
      size: size,
      first_bytes: for(<<b <- binary_part(content, 0, min(size, 16))>>, do: Base.encode16(<<b>>)) |> Enum.join(" ")
    }

    [section | read(rest, at + 1 + length + size)]
  end

  # An unsigned LEB128 number: the number, the bytes after it, and its length.
  defp uleb(bytes, shift \\ 0, acc \\ 0, length \\ 1)

  defp uleb(<<1::1, low::7, rest::binary>>, shift, acc, length),
    do: uleb(rest, shift + 7, acc ||| low <<< shift, length + 1)

  defp uleb(<<0::1, low::7, rest::binary>>, shift, acc, length),
    do: {acc ||| low <<< shift, rest, length}
end

WasmBytes.sections(bytes)
```

## Call the functions

`wasm:compile/1` validates the bytes. `wasm:instantiate/1` makes an instance: the memory, and the functions that the cells can call.

```elixir
{:ok, module} = :wasm.compile(bytes)
{:ok, instance} = :wasm.instantiate(module)

%{
  add: :wasm.call_function(instance, "add", [2, 3]),
  fact: :wasm.call_function(instance, "fact", [20]),
  exists: {:wasm.function_exists(instance, "fact"), :wasm.function_exists(instance, "nope")}
}
```

20! is 2432902008176640000. It is larger than 2<sup>53</sup>, the largest integer that JavaScript keeps exactly as a number. The `i64` values go between the VM and the engine as text, so the value is exact.

Bad calls give errors, not a crash of the VM. A division by zero is a trap:

```elixir
%{
  trap: :wasm.call_function(instance, "divide", [1, 0]),
  not_found: :wasm.call_function(instance, "nope", []),
  after_the_trap: :wasm.call_function(instance, "divide", [100, 7])
}
```

## The memory

The memory of an instance is an array of bytes. The data section put a text at the offset 0:

```elixir
:wasm.read_binary(instance, 0, 15)
```

The cell writes the bytes 1 to 100 at the offset 1024. Then `sum` reads them in its loop:

```elixir
:ok = :wasm.write_binary(instance, 1024, :binary.list_to_bin(Enum.to_list(1..100)))
:wasm.call_function(instance, "sum", [1024, 100])
```

The memory grows by pages of 64 KiB. `wasm:memory_grow/2` and the instruction `memory.grow` give the size before, in pages:

```elixir
{:ok, before} = :wasm.memory_size(instance)
{:ok, [old_pages]} = :wasm.call_function(instance, "pages", [1])
{:ok, now} = :wasm.memory_size(instance)
%{bytes_before: before, pages_before: old_pages, bytes_now: now}
```

## A C program with WASI

WASI (the WebAssembly System Interface) gives a program its arguments, its environment, the clock and the output. This C program was compiled with Emscripten (`emcc -O2 -sSTANDALONE_WASM hello.c -o hello.wasm`):

```elixir
Kino.FS.file_path("hello.c") |> File.read!() |> IO.puts()
```

Its module imports only WASI functions:

```elixir
hello = Kino.FS.file_path("hello.wasm") |> File.read!()

hello
|> WasmBytes.sections()
|> Enum.map(&Map.take(&1, [:section, :size]))
```

`wasm:run/2` makes an instance and calls its function `_start`. The first argument is the name of the program. The program writes to the output of this cell, and gives its exit code. With more than one argument, it gives 3:

```elixir
:wasm.run(hello, %{args: ["hello", "one", "two"], env: %{"WHO" => "Livebook"}})
```

## The same calls in Erlang

Livebook also runs Erlang cells. An Erlang cell sees the variables of the Elixir cells above, with a capital letter: `Hello` is `hello`, the bytes of the C program. This cell runs the program again, in Erlang:

```erlang
{ok, Wasi} = wasm:instantiate(Hello, #{}, #{args => ["hello"], env => #{"WHO" => "Erlang"}}),
{wasm:function_exists(Wasi, "_start"), wasm:start(Wasi)}.
```

## The limits

In the WebAssembly runtime, the module `wasm` has these limits:

* A module can import only WASI functions. Host functions (Erlang functions that the module calls) are not supported. This is also true in the native `beam.com`.
* A WASI program has no files (no preopens), and its standard input is empty.
* The output of a program comes when the call returns, not while it runs.
* The code of a module runs on the thread of the host. A function that does not return stops the VM.
* A Cloudflare Worker cannot compile WebAssembly at run time. There, `wasm:compile/1` gives an error. A web page and Deno can compile.

